# shortest-path.zd - Dijkstra over a weighted graph, and the priority
# queue that is not there.
#
# The cheapest route is not the shortest one. From Ashford to Girvan the
# fewest-roads route is Ashford, Coleford, Enderby, Girvan: three roads and
# a toll of 19. The cheapest is Ashford, Coleford, Broadwell, Denby,
# Enderby, Fenwick, Girvan: six roads and a toll of 14. A breadth-first
# walk finds the first and is wrong about the question this file asks,
# which is why this is a separate example and not a flag on the last one.
#
# THE GAP THIS FILE IS EVIDENCE FOR. Dijkstra is a priority queue and
# nothing else. There is no heap in the prelude and no way to write one
# that is better than a scan, so `cheapestCost` walks the whole frontier
# to find the smallest toll, `firstAtCost` walks it again to find where
# that entry is, and `removeAt` copies it a third time to take the entry
# out. Three linear passes per extraction, against a binary heap's one
# logarithmic one. On seven towns that is invisible. It is written down
# because it is the reason there are seven.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT WORKED AROUND. The frontier holds stale
# entries: when a cheaper route to a town is found, the dearer one is left
# in place and skipped when it surfaces. That is lazy deletion and it is
# what a real implementation does too, because decrease-key needs a handle
# into the heap. Here it is not a choice: with no map there is nowhere to
# keep a handle. `pops` counts every extraction including the skipped
# ones, so the page shows what the missing structure costs.
#
# TWO MORE THINGS, BOTH SINCE ANSWERED, AND NEITHER CHANGES THE GAP ABOVE.
#
# `setAt` did not exist when this was written, so a distance table indexed
# by town could not be updated in place even if the frontier were one, and
# the settled towns are a list that is appended to and read back by
# scanning. It exists now (#195), and that scan could be a table. It is
# left as it is deliberately: the extraction cost this file is evidence for
# is the frontier's, not the settled set's, and rewriting the settled set
# would change what the page measures without closing the gap it measures.
#
# `from` and `to` are still keywords, so a road's ends are still `tail` and
# `head`. What #199 asked for was not the names — it explicitly did not ask
# for keywords to be legal in field position — but a diagnostic that says
# the rule instead of only naming the token. That shipped:
#
#     Error: Expected a name as a field name. No keyword may be a name:
#     `from` introduces a pipeline's source, and derives state from other
#     state.
#      2 │     from is Whole
#        │     ──┬─
#        │       ╰─── `from` is a keyword, so it cannot be a name
#        │ Help: run 'zdc explain E0102' for the rule
#
# `tail` and `head` is the graph theorist's spelling and reads fine once
# chosen. It is still not what anyone reaches for first, which is why the
# rename is recorded here rather than passed over in silence.

record Road
    tail is Whole
    head is Whole
    toll is Whole

# One reached town: how dear it was to get there, and which town it was
# reached through. `via` is what makes the route reconstructible without a
# predecessor map, which is the other thing this language has no way to
# build.
record Reach
    node is Whole
    cost is Whole
    via  is Whole

# The search itself: what is final, what is still tentative, and how many
# entries have been taken out of the frontier so far.
record Search
    settled is List of Reach
    border  is List of Reach
    pops    is Whole
    done    is Truth

state towns is client List of Text starting ["Ashford", "Broadwell", "Coleford", "Denby", "Enderby", "Fenwick", "Girvan"]
state places is client List of Whole from range with start is 0, stop is 7

# Eleven roads. A collection literal may not span lines.
state roads is client List of Road starting [(Road with tail is 0, head is 1, toll is 4), (Road with tail is 0, head is 2, toll is 2), (Road with tail is 1, head is 2, toll is 1), (Road with tail is 1, head is 3, toll is 5), (Road with tail is 2, head is 3, toll is 8), (Road with tail is 2, head is 4, toll is 10), (Road with tail is 3, head is 4, toll is 2), (Road with tail is 3, head is 5, toll is 6), (Road with tail is 4, head is 5, toll is 3), (Road with tail is 5, head is 6, toll is 1), (Road with tail is 4, head is 6, toll is 7)]

# Where the traveller is going. The one signal a click moves.
state target is client Whole starting 6

# --- the graph -----------------------------------------------------------

function otherSide with road, node
    if road.tail is node
        give road.head
    give road.tail

# Every town one road away from `node`, each priced at what it costs to
# stand in `node` plus the road's own toll. This is the relaxation, and it
# is a pipeline because a pipeline is exactly "apply this to every
# element".
#
# The `map each … to` clause builds the record in place. It used to go
# through a `reachFrom` function that existed only to be a call rather
# than a literal, because the emitter wrote a bare `{ node: …, cost: … }`
# into the arrow body and the bundle was a JavaScript syntax error that
# `check` and `build` both exited 0 on. That is issue #194, found here.
function stepsFrom with roads, node, cost
    from roads
    keep each road where (road.tail is node) or (road.head is node)
    map each road to (Reach with node is (otherSide with road is road, node is node), cost is cost + road.toll, via is node)

function followedBy with before, after
    give flatten of [before, after]

# --- the priority queue that is a scan ------------------------------------

function costsIn of border
    from border
    map each reach to reach.cost

# The smallest toll anywhere in the frontier, or `None` when the frontier
# is empty. First of the three passes.
function cheapestCost of border
    give minOf of (costsIn of border)

# Where that toll sits. Second pass. A heap would have handed back the
# entry and its position together; two separate walks is what it costs to
# ask the same question with `minOf` and then `listAt`.
function firstAtCost with border, cost, index
    when listAt with value is border, index is index
        None
            give 0
        Some with reach
            if reach.cost is cost
                give index
            give firstAtCost with border is border, cost is cost, index is index + 1

# --- the settled set that is a scan too -----------------------------------

# The final entry for `node`, if it has one. `Option` rather than a
# sentinel toll, because "unreachable" and "reachable at cost zero" are
# different answers and a number cannot hold both.
function entryFor with settled, node, index
    when listAt with value is settled, index is index
        None
            give None
        Some with reach
            if reach.node is node
                give Some with value is reach
            give entryFor with settled is settled, node is node, index is index + 1

function isSettled with settled, node
    give isSome of (entryFor with settled is settled, node is node, index is 0)

function costTo with settled, node
    when entryFor with settled is settled, node is node, index is 0
        None
            give 0 - 1
        Some with reach
            give reach.cost

# --- one extraction -------------------------------------------------------

function settleOne with roads, hunt
    when cheapestCost of hunt.border
        None
            give Search with settled is hunt.settled, border is empty, pops is hunt.pops, done is yes
        Some with cheapest
            give settleAt with roads is roads, hunt is hunt, cheapestAt is (firstAtCost with border is hunt.border, cost is cheapest, index is 0)

# The entry is taken out whether it is used or not, so a stale one costs
# an iteration and never loops.
function settleAt with roads, hunt, cheapestAt
    when listAt with value is hunt.border, index is cheapestAt
        None
            give Search with settled is hunt.settled, border is empty, pops is hunt.pops, done is yes
        Some with reach
            with others is (removeAt with items is hunt.border, index is cheapestAt)
            if isSettled with settled is hunt.settled, node is reach.node
                give Search with settled is hunt.settled, border is others, pops is hunt.pops + 1, done is no
            give Search with settled is (append reach to hunt.settled), border is (followedBy with before is others, after is (stepsFrom with roads is roads, node is reach.node, cost is reach.cost)), pops is hunt.pops + 1, done is no

# `fuel` bounds the run rather than describing it: every road contributes
# at most one frontier entry per settlement, so twice the road count plus
# the start is an upper bound on the extractions and anything above that
# is slack. The loop stops on `done` first.
function dijkstra with roads, start, fuel
    give settleAll with roads is roads, hunt is (Search with settled is empty, border is (append (Reach with node is start, cost is 0, via is start) to empty), pops is 0, done is no), fuel is fuel

function settleAll with roads, hunt, fuel
    if fuel < 1
        give hunt
    if hunt.done
        give hunt
    give settleAll with roads is roads, hunt is (settleOne with roads is roads, hunt is hunt), fuel is fuel - 1

# --- reading the route back ----------------------------------------------

# The towns from `start` to `node`, in order. Built backwards, because
# `via` points the other way, and turned round once at the end.
function legsTo with settled, node, start, trail
    with sofar is (append node to trail)
    if node is start
        give reverse of sofar
    when entryFor with settled is settled, node is node, index is 0
        None
            give reverse of sofar
        Some with reach
            give legsTo with settled is settled, node is reach.via, start is start, trail is sofar

function nameOf with towns, node
    give valueOr with maybe is (listAt with value is towns, index is node), fallback is "nowhere"

function namesOf with towns, legs
    from legs
    map each node to (nameOf with towns is towns, node is node)

# --- what the page shows -------------------------------------------------

state found is client Search from dijkstra with roads is roads, start is 0, fuel is 64

state legs      is client List of Whole from legsTo with settled is found.settled, node is target, start is 0, trail is empty
state legsText  is client Text from join with parts is (namesOf with towns is towns, legs is legs), using is " to "
state tollPaid  is client Whole from costTo with settled is found.settled, node is target
state extractions is client Whole from found.pops
state targetName is client Text from nameOf with towns is towns, node is target

view
    Column
        Heading "The cheapest way there"
        Text "Seven towns, eleven roads, every road priced. Pick a destination."

        Row
            each place in places
                Button (nameOf with towns is towns, node is place)
                    on click
                        set target to place

        Column padding is 8
            Row
                Text "going to "
                Text targetName, weight is "bold"
            Row
                Text "route: "
                Text legsText
            Row
                Text "toll: "
                Text tollPaid, weight is "bold"

        Column padding is 8
            Heading "the whole tree"
            Text "What one run of the search settles: the cheapest toll to every town, from Ashford."
            each place in places
                Row
                    Text (nameOf with towns is towns, node is place)
                    Text " costs "
                    Text (costTo with settled is found.settled, node is place)

        Column padding is 8
            Heading "what the missing heap costs"
            Row
                Text "entries taken out of the frontier: "
                Text extractions, weight is "bold"
            Text "Seven of those settled a town. The rest were stale routes left behind because there is no way to reach into the frontier and lower a price, and each of them still cost three passes over the whole frontier to find, identify and remove.", color is "grey"
